1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,200
Welcome to all of you, can everybody see and hear me?... Good...

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:09,860
I'm Sally, your guide for this tour of the Bicentennial Park...

3
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:12,980
I hope that you're all wearing your most comfortable shoes

4
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:14,600
and that you can keep up the pace.

5
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:19,240
So let's get under way on our tour around this wonderful park.

6
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:23,400
I'll start today with some general background information.

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:28,200
There used to be a lot of factories in this area until the 1960s.

8
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:34,090
Creating the park required the demolition of lots of derelict buildings on the site,

9
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:40,400
so most of the exciting park space all around you was originally warehouses and storehouses.

10
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:45,460
The idea of building a public park here was first discussed

11
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:49,500
when a property developer proposed a high-rise housing development,

12
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,230
but the local community wasn't happy.

13
00:00:52,230 --> 00:00:58,670
If the land was to be cleaned up, they wanted to use the site for recreation.

14
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:00,920
Residents wanted open space for outdoor activities

15
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:05,110
rather than housing or even an indoor sports complex.

16
00:01:05,110 --> 00:01:09,190
Now to the Bicentennial Park itself.

17
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:12,500
It has two areas, a nature reserve

18
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:16,000
and a formal park with man-made features and gardens.

19
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,286
The tall blue, and-white building in front of us is called The Tower

20
00:01:20,286 --> 00:01:23,500
and is the centre point for the formal gardens.

21
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:26,380
It stands twelve metres high

22
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:31,900
so follow me up the stairs to where we can take advantage of the fantastic views.

23
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:37,640
Well, here we are at the top of The Tower

24
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:40,630
and we're going to look at the view from each direction.

25
00:01:40,630 --> 00:01:46,720
Out to the east, the large buildings about a kilometre away are on the Olympic site.

26
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:49,212
There's an indoor arena for gymnastics,

27
00:01:49,212 --> 00:01:53,800
a stadium for track and field and a swimming pool for races

28
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:56,690
and synchronised swimming and also diving.

29
00:01:56,690 --> 00:02:01,860
If you look carefully down there, you can see the train lines.

30
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:06,500
The Olympic site has its own station to encourage the use of public transport.

31
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:10,860
There is also a car park, but it only holds a limited number of cars.

32
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:15,000
The formal park has some specially-created water features.

33
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,500
If you look out here to the south, you can see a circular ornamental pond.

34
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:22,920
And around to the west

35
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:27,108
you can relax and sit on a bench to smell the flowers in the rose garden,

36
00:02:27,108 --> 00:02:32,458
and finally up to the north, if you look in front of you now, there's a lake

37
00:02:32,458 --> 00:02:37,220
with a small island in the centre, you can hire rowing boats at the boat shed

38
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:39,381
which you can't see from here,

39
00:02:39,381 --> 00:02:41,400
but if you look through the trees

40
00:02:41,540 --> 00:02:45,360
you can see the café, which has lovely views across the water.

41
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:48,460
OK, let's climb down now.

42
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:52,090
We will go now and have a look at the nature reserve section,

43
00:02:52,090 --> 00:02:55,440
of the park, which has opened up natural wetland to the public.

44
00:02:55,440 --> 00:03:02,150
The Mangroves have been made more accessible to visitors by the boardwalk built during the park's upgrade.

45
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:07,105
You'd think that people would come here to look at the unusual plant life of the area,

46
00:03:07,105 --> 00:03:09,520
but in fact it's more often used for cycling

47
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:12,060
and is very popular with the local clubs.

48
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:17,437
This is the far end of the park and over there you can see the Frog Pond,

49
00:03:17,437 --> 00:03:20,600
a natural feature here long before the park was designed.

50
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:26,260
Just next to it we have our outdoor classroom, a favourite spot for school parties.

51
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:31,180
The area is now most often used by primary schools for biology lessons.

52
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:35,720
And finally let's pass by the Waterbird Refuge.

53
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:39,059
This area is in a sheltered part of the estuary,

54
00:03:39,059 --> 00:03:43,399
that's why the park's viewing shelter is a favourite spot for bird watchers

55
00:03:43,399 --> 00:03:46,070
who can use it to spy through binoculars.

56
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:48,699
You can watch a variety of water birds,

57
00:03:48,699 --> 00:03:53,300
but most visitors expect to see black swans when they come to the shelter.

58
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:56,000
You might spot one yourself right now!

59
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,600
Well, here we are back at our starting point, the Visitor Centre.
